<p>A cluster of recent reports has put Iran’s Kharg Island—an offshore terminal in the northern Gulf that handles nearly all of the country’s crude exports—back in the global spotlight, as analysts weigh whether the US and Israel might broaden their military campaign to include the site. Multiple outlets, citing Axios, say Trump administration officials have discussed capturing Kharg as a possible option—an idea analysts describe as a sharp escalation. So far, Kharg’s export infrastructure has not been hit, even as some voices argue for striking it and others warn that taking the terminal offline could jolt already-rising oil prices and send shockwaves through the global economy. Separate feature reporting also underscores Kharg’s unusual dual identity: an island with ancient ruins and tight security, sitting beside the nerve center of Iran’s oil-export system—an economic lifeline that shapes both Iran’s resilience and the world’s energy anxieties.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/c6x-gbl_tJBZKLoC7WN_0C_-mMED-JX4hRcZ88J_hI1SzpvckS7Vt1JNoxO8F5_hiXvfbByHxMeTEePK2Vle_vTA0LgALB1v8A0YpDH9-M7A8Cxzi-TYGEUEKoBg9lIrh2SzVOYfyQYG2F1dWMylaesB52l_xySM30Dk35vQu8NgWRCqnvIRwQSkT2O1Oz3up8jXMpVT5MaN1ScbdqGM8alD74B-hqBaKt2bYW7G_r0gK_qAVXCtVuw1uSGzOLQRq2aWCLl_kUpwpjt8pfUgeKc' alt='Kharg Island, Iran’s main crude-export terminal, has remained untouched in the campaign described by The Guardian.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Throughput estimate: The Guardian estimates about 90% of Iran’s oil exports flow through Kharg, putting a number on the broader “nearly all” descriptions.</li><li>Geography and profile: Several accounts describe Kharg as a scrubby island in the northern Gulf, about 30 km (19 miles) off Iran’s mainland.</li><li>Capture option vs airstrikes: In the Axios-cited reporting, the idea under discussion is capturing Kharg—cast as a different (and more escalatory) step than launching airstrikes on infrastructure.</li><li>‘Forbidden Island’ identity: Al Jazeera’s feature adds color, portraying Kharg as an “orphan pearl” with ancient ruins and restricted access alongside heavily protected oil facilities.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration officials (as cited by Axios): Officials told Axios that capturing Kharg Island was “on the table” as the war persists. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3346181/irans-vital-oil-hub-kharg-island-americas-crosshairs'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Energy-market experts and commentators: Some argue for destroying the export terminal, while others warn it could send the global market into a “tailspin” by taking Iran’s exports offline. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/11/why-irans-vital-kharg-island-oil-hub-is-still-untouched-by-us-israel-bombers'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/3/11/the-orphan-pearl-inside-kharg-the-beating-heart-of-irans-oil-empire'>The ‘orphan pearl’: Inside Kharg, the beating heart of Iran’s oil empire</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/kharg-island-irans-vital-oil-hub-in-the-crosshairs'>Kharg Island: Iran’s vital oil hub in the crosshairs?</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3346181/irans-vital-oil-hub-kharg-island-americas-crosshairs'>Is Iran’s vital oil hub Kharg Island in America’s crosshairs?</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/kharg-island-irans-vital-oil-hub-crosshairs'>Kharg Island: Iran&#x27;s vital oil hub in the crosshairs?</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/11/why-irans-vital-kharg-island-oil-hub-is-still-untouched-by-us-israel-bombers'>Why Iran’s vital Kharg Island oil hub is still untouched by US-Israel bombers</a> - theguardian.com</li></ul>